WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.031
Thomas Malthus,

00:00:01.031 --> 00:00:03.271
a famous 18th century

00:00:03.351 --> 00:00:07.591
economist, famously wrote that most of

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.791
human history there was no growth.

00:00:09.791 --> 00:00:11.321
In fact there could not be any growth.

00:00:11.321 --> 00:00:13.481
Because as soon as you started to make

00:00:13.481 --> 00:00:16.571
more food, what would happen?

00:00:16.571 --> 00:00:19.371
Your children would survive. You'd have more sex.

00:00:19.471 --> 00:00:23.131
Have more children. As we all do when we

00:00:23.141 --> 00:00:27.131
eat too much. It was the 18th century,

00:00:27.131 --> 00:00:31.271
things change. Then suddenly gobble

00:00:31.271 --> 00:00:35.140
up all the excess food. So that's

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.440
what this graph is. This graph of world GDP

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.081
per capita. This is the graph

00:00:41.081 --> 00:00:43.780
over here. It's not world GDP, which is a

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.541
measure of all the economic activity, but

00:00:46.541 --> 00:00:50.651
the world GDP per person. Now I know that,

00:00:50.651 --> 00:00:52.541
if you're anything like my history students

00:00:52.541 --> 00:00:55.091
at Cornell, you hate math. But a little

00:00:55.091 --> 00:00:57.731
math goes a long way. So how

00:00:57.731 --> 00:01:00.401
manyof you are frightened of math

00:01:00.401 --> 00:01:05.351
Well, San Francisco is a different crowd.

00:01:05.351 --> 00:01:08.351
Y'all are like... okay, math, it's on! All right.

00:01:08.351 --> 00:01:11.711
So if you look at this graph... I want you

00:01:11.711 --> 00:01:14.561
to look at the year 0, or 1, or whatever

00:01:14.561 --> 00:01:18.851
$467. Now, four hundred sixty-seven

00:01:18.851 --> 00:01:20.621
dollars per person in the whole planet, now,

00:01:20.621 --> 00:01:22.091
do you think that's totally accurate?

00:01:22.091 --> 00:01:23.621
No, probably not, but it gives you a rough--

00:01:23.621 --> 00:01:26.680
woah! Don't be touching my beer.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.511
Okay, this is a rough sense...

00:01:31.511 --> 00:01:37.841
Go to 1000 AD: $450. Roughly flat.

00:01:37.841 --> 00:01:40.091
If you extend this graph back thousands and

00:01:40.091 --> 00:01:42.790
thousands of years, roughly it would have

00:01:42.790 --> 00:01:45.851
stayed flat. That's basically

00:01:45.851 --> 00:01:49.331
the amount of human activity per person since

00:01:49.331 --> 00:01:52.661
the dawn of Agriculture and then

00:01:52.661 --> 00:01:56.500
something changes. Something changes to

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.741
make Malthus incorrect. And like

00:01:59.741 --> 00:02:01.811
most historians he imagines the future

00:02:01.811 --> 00:02:03.971
through the past. So Malthus is looking

00:02:03.971 --> 00:02:06.011
back. And if that had been the case,

00:02:06.011 --> 00:02:08.231
if nothing had changed Malthus would

00:02:08.231 --> 00:02:12.191
had been correct. Before capitalism,

00:02:12.191 --> 00:02:14.261
for the 99.7%

00:02:14.261 --> 00:02:16.721
of all human history there was very

00:02:16.721 --> 00:02:19.300
little investment there was very

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:22.691
little technological change the world of 01450

00:02:22.691 --> 00:02:24.701
looked more or less like a world of

00:02:24.701 --> 00:02:27.581
the year 0. These printers and glass blowers

00:02:27.581 --> 00:02:30.461
looked like technologies that would have

00:02:30.461 --> 00:02:32.291
been legible to the ancient Romans.

00:02:32.291 --> 00:02:34.901
And in fact the largest enterprises

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:37.571
in Europe in this period had 40 people.

00:02:37.571 --> 00:02:40.630
That's the scale of human endeavor

00:02:40.631 --> 00:02:43.091
in the moment before capitalism really

00:02:43.091 --> 00:02:47.411
emerges. And then suddenly things change.

00:02:47.691 --> 00:02:51.571
Things change. And you get to this graph.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:54.451
This very smooth looking graph.

00:02:54.471 --> 00:02:56.411
Now I notice some of you are nodding your head.

00:02:56.411 --> 00:02:59.171
What do you notice, class, about the

00:02:59.171 --> 00:03:02.741
math of this graph? I want you to notice

00:03:02.741 --> 00:03:06.851
the very smooth flat plane until roughly

00:03:06.851 --> 00:03:08.651
the end. Now, it looks very flat because

00:03:08.651 --> 00:03:12.371
the end, the period post 01800, is

00:03:12.371 --> 00:03:18.221
so vertical it's very easy to draw this

00:03:18.221 --> 00:03:23.020
graph. Even a robot can do it.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.911
And it would be easy to forget that behind

00:03:24.911 --> 00:03:28.871
this graph are people and events and

00:03:28.871 --> 00:03:31.751
choices that made this growth possible. That

00:03:31.751 --> 00:03:34.031
in fact this kind of growth wasn't

00:03:34.031 --> 00:03:36.511
possible at any other period in the hundreds of

00:03:36.511 --> 00:03:38.891
thousands of years that humans have existed.

00:03:47.491 --> 00:03:49.551
you

